Liora led him to Kael's workshop. "First, you must look," she said, gesturing to the mirror.

Vex saw himself drowning in a sea of faces—every soldier he'd commanded into death, every family displaced by his ambitions. The weight pulled him under, endless and suffocating.

He staggered back, gasping. "How do you bear it? Seeing what you've done?"

"By emptying myself of excuses," Liora said quietly. "When I was full of justifications and pride, there was no room for truth. I had to become hollow—no defenses, no stories. Just space for what actually was."

"And then?" Vex whispered.

"Then you begin filling that emptiness differently. Not with conquests, but with repairs. Not with trophies, but with relationships. The mirror doesn't change what you did—it changes what you choose to do next."

Vex removed his medals that day, one by one. Each discarded decoration created space—not for shame, but for the person he might become if he stopped defending who he'd been.

Sometimes transformation requires becoming empty enough to be remade.

